nd.\n\n**Comparison (expected values, mental estimates):**\n- **Item 0, k = 1.0 vs 1.1:** going to 1.1 adds about 121 units of purchases (−1090) and storage (about −520). It saves about 60 units of shortfall at 26 each (+1560). Net about −50, effectively a tie.\n- **Item 1, k = 1.0 vs 1.1:** going to 1.1 adds about 91 units of purchases (−1090) and storage (about −540). It saves about 45 units of shortfall at 24 each (+1080). Net about −550, so k = 1.0 is better.\n- **Warehouse capacity:** by days 24–25, candidate 5 is carrying about 150 units of surplus. When the 101 units of item 1 arrive, total stock exceeds 236 and some of the delivery is lost. Candidate 0 stays at about 190 units at that point.\n- **Candidates 6, 7 and 4:** each extra unit of item 0 beyond k = 1.1 costs 9 plus storage and saves very little shortage. These come out about 800–2000 worse than candidate 0, with candidate 4 the worst.\n- **Candidate 2 (k = 0.9):** large shortfalls. Shortage costs 17–26 per unit for item 0 and 12–24 for item 1, so it loses about 1900 against candidate 0.\n- **Candidate 3:** it never orders item 1, so it pays a penalty of 6 on about 950 units of demand and earns nothing from item 1. Clearly worst.\n- **Candidate 1:** ordering every second day saves about 280 in daily order fees. But it stores an extra day's demand every other night, about 56 per two-day cycle. Net it is about 400 worse than candidate 5, and therefore worse than candidate 0.\n\n**Robustness:** candidate 0 builds only a small surplus, so it is less exposed to the capacity limit and to stock spoiling at the end. Its weak spot is a run of high-demand days, which shows up as extra shortfalls. The other candidates lose at least as much from over-ordering."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 3784, "cache_read_input_tokens": 2269, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_inp